# Children of the Five Winds — The Qamarist Caliphate

**Version:** 0.4.1
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings: Children of the Five Winds
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## The Ganzu of the Hidden Valley

As denizens of the most valuable oasis in the Burning Sands, the Ganzu people are among the Unicorn's most important allies along the Sand Road. As vassals of the Shinjo family and allies of the Moto, the Ganzu protect the Hidden Valley from those who would seize its bounty for themselves. They are a people of the crossroads, exploring the world as it comes to them from either end of the Sand Road.

### Customs

The Ganzu people have little reason to venture beyond the fertile river valley of their home. Vast deserts of sand and sunbaked clay await them in all directions. For protection from the unrelenting heat, Ganzu homes are mostly underground, and even the tallest spires of their city reach to just beneath the tops of the canyon walls. From the horizon, the city is invisible; none would suspect the thriving civilization just beneath the canyon lip until they were upon it.

The Ganzu people have much of their cultural history in common with the Ujik tribes of the steppes and Burning Sands. However, as time has passed, they have assimilated the cultural artifacts that have passed through the Hidden Valley. Their homes display a mix of eastern and western sensibilities, and their cultural norms are syncretic with both Ujik and al-Zawiran customs. Many Ganzu have embraced the Qamarist religion; temples to the one God of the Nameless Prophet dot the hidden city. But they still tell the folk stories of their old religions, and their old gods are seen through the lens of new beliefs. They see no contradiction in the marriage of the Word of the Prophet and a continued belief in the Lords of Death or in the wisdom of the Little Teacher.

### Arts

As peoples of an important desert crossroads, the Ganzu have adapted many arts into their repertoire. Among the most important is dance: folk dances are a cultural touchstone and pastime, and many religious dances are practiced throughout the year. Another revered art is that of the storyteller: the bard enjoys a high position of cultural importance. The bard must have a vast repository of stories memorized and ready to present at a moment's notice. During seated performances, the Ganzu bard employs props and shadow puppetry to further enhance their tales, which are commonly based on historical events, local folklore, or legend.

### Defense

The Hidden Valley is the only oasis between al-Zawira and Khanbulak along the Sand Road, and thus it is a tempting target for any who wish to seize control of the route. This happened once during the Second Caliphate War, and the Ganzu have taken their defeat to heart. Ganzu warriors are primarily known for martial skill with axes, the ax being regarded as an important and versatile tool in their culture, the warrior's tie to the farmer and logger.

## The Qamarist Caliphate

When the Unicorn passed through the Cradle of the World, they left the ruin of an empire behind them. The old Empire of Rempet, a kingdom of enslaving sorcerer-kings, made an enemy of the Unicorn and its own populace and paid the ultimate price. But the fall of the wicked pharaohs did not lead immediately to an age of peace and prosperity for their former subjects. Indeed, chaos followed as warlords struggled to replace the kings of old and the varied tribes of the region fought against one another.

Into this unsettled land came peace, in the form of a prophet. Although history does not record his name (in an effort to protect him and his spirit from the depredations of sorcerers and name magic) or even his origin, it does record his many teachings and words of wisdom in a collection of writings known as the Word of the Prophet. The Prophet himself was a charismatic leader, one who began to unify the many disparate peoples of the Cradle of the World. His successors, the Caliphs, have continued to support the Word's spread over the past few centuries, and now the Caliphate rivals the Empire of Rempet for scope and power—it is a kingdom united not by blood or culture or even language, but by the Word.

### REPUTATION AND VALUES

The Qamari people are diverse, yet they still present something of a united front to outsiders. Their different societies have certain commonalities—such as a universal commitment to hospitality common to many cultures in harsh climates—and their faithful obedience to the Word offers them still more unity. To the rest of the world, the Qamarists are known as honest, hospitable, and forthright. They have a well-earned reputation as great scholars and thinkers on a vast array of subjects, ranging from theology to natural philosophy to mathematics.

The Qamari openness and welcoming nature do not extend, however, to sorcerers, and particularly not to those who use name magic, such as was favored in the Empire of Rempet. Nor do they favor those who enslave others or who consort with demons and monsters—in short, those who engage in any of the common practices or trappings of the ancient empire against which their forebears rose in rebellion. Indeed, the only practice of the Empire of Rempet the Qamarists have willingly inherited is the never-ending war against the Drowned God, Zeth, and the monsters

that emerge from the Cursed Sea. (That many of the technological, architectural, and agricultural practices now refined in the Caliphate were originally developed under the Empire of Rempet is conveniently ignored. Deliberate mimicry of the great tombs and pyramids of the sorcerer-kings or any of their other famous symbols remains taboo.) The Prophet commands that everyone should oppose works of evil, and the Qamarists are absolutely united in this regard—although sometimes opinions differ as to what form, precisely, evil may take.

The diversity of the Caliphate carries with it a diversity of opinion and reputation. The dark-haired Nehiri, who first embraced the Word and have ever stood as foremost among the Caliphate, are famously friendly and polite, great welcomers of pilgrims, and enthusiastic proselytizers of the Word. The grey-eyed Suhili of the northern plains are famously courageous and fierce, bold warriors who battle endlessly against the monsters of Zeth from horseback or their terrible scythe-wheeled chariots. The Sogdans, with their meticulously curled hair, are renowned as gloomy and dour, but honest and clear-spoken to the point of brusqueness. Their marvelous city-states are plagued by a rivalry that transcends their common faith in the Word, which the Sogdans attribute to a curse from God for daring to build too high. And the deep-skinned Bandar, the most recent group to join the Caliphate, are divided into the inland Bandar, with their herds and hunters, and the coastal Bandar, who are the most skilled sailors on the Sea of Jewels. Both sorts are famously quick-witted and daring, and Bandar merchants can be found anywhere a boat can sail or a camel can roam.

The same beliefs that unify the Qamarists and encourage them to be so hospitable and friendly can at times cause tension or even suffering for their neighbors. The Prophet's Word is for everyone, not to be limited by borders or divisions of culture or creed. While this, on the one hand, means that all humanity are kin to be treated as one family, it can also give the Caliphate all the excuse it needs to become an expansionist empire. At various times in the Caliphate's history, the Word has been spread as much by the sword as by the book. Furthermore, while the general interpretation of the Prophet's will has been to tolerate differences of religious

opinion, not all religious teachers (and perhaps more importantly, political leaders) have always agreed on this score. Nasrenes, Saborim, and the occasional Shinseist within the Caliphate have been persecuted in the past. As the current caliph is said to be both ambitious and not especially devout, some foresee a new wave of conflict ahead, one that entails both great risks and possible opportunities for believers in foreign faiths.

The Cradle of the World embraces the people of many tribes and cultures. While it is easy enough to tell brown-skinned Bandar from the pale, fell Suhili, to the Qamari, these obvious differences are of little import. The true identifying factor, the question whose answer tells the listener what they really need to know, is, "What is your religion?"

There are, after all, Nehiri who are Nasrene, Nehiri who are Saborim, even a few unrepentant sorcerers and heretics. They cannot necessarily be distinguished from the Qamarist Nehiri by sight or sound. Nor does the color of a Bandar's skin or the fine jewelry in a Sogdan's beard tell the viewer anything about their beliefs or character. But knowing their religion? With that information, a person can finally be placed in the world.

Because the Prophet spoke and wrote in Nehiri, that tongue has become the most important in the Caliphate. New converts to Qamarism are encouraged to learn it so that they can read the Prophet's words in their original form. It helps, of course, that Nehiri was already widespread as a trade language in the region.

Traditionally, each Qamarist is expected to read and interpret the Word for themself. Literacy, therefore, is highly prized and near-universal throughout the Caliphate. Teaching a child or a convert to read, and thereafter to interpret the will of the Prophet, is considered a good work, and many schools have been established for this purpose. These schools serve as gathering places for theological scholars, who often engage in rigorous debate as to the Prophet's meaning in a given passage or even his reasoning behind a certain pronouncement—for example, did the Prophet warn against the excesses of drunkenness in general when he forbade his followers to drink of the "fruit of the grape or fig," or should his proscription be interpreted only in reference to those specific forms of wine? And for that matter, were his pronouncements for his immediate servants ever meant to be applied to the general population? (All matters of keen interest to Rokugani merchants eager to export sake!)

This scholarly tradition encourages a certain amount of free thinking and even rebelliousness, at least when compared to Rokugan. The emirs of the Caliphate (who occupy a position similar to that of daimyō) have risen up and replaced more than one caliph in the past, and even the need for the caliph's role has been argued. (The first religious schism in the Qamarist faith occurred when the Saborim rejected the first caliph and all his successors, preferring to await the literal return of the Prophet.) Central tenets of Qamarism have been revised and reformed due to this scholarly, free-thinking tendency. For example, the faith as initially practiced was polytheistic, acknowledging individual gods for the sun, moon, and other natural phenomena. But theologians who truly studied the meaning of the Prophet's words came to realize that, in fact, all these gods were but reflections or manifestations of the one true God, who has not one name but many. Current trends of thought suggest that perhaps even the Enemy exists at the sufferance of God, for the temptation and testing of humans and djinn.

Consistent throughout this evolving thought have been the Four Virtues that the Prophet wished his followers to emphasize: Hospitality, Generosity, Temperance, and the Destruction of Evil. The Qamarists must always be willing to support and assist their kin in faith, and even the whole of humankind. They must always remain masters of their own minds and apply their judgment wisely and soundly to the challenges of life. And they must oppose evil in all its forms to defend God's creation against the forces of chaos and to improve the plight of all humanity.

Yet these common understandings of the Word are filtered through the existing cultures of the people in the Cradle of the World. The Nehiri, of course, take the virtue of Hospitality to be the root of all the Prophet's teachings, for what is hospitality if not welcoming another as one would welcome a sibling into one's home? And is not the true home of Qamarism the whole world? The Suhili, for their part, were already battling against the forces of evil when the Word came to them—Destruction of Evil is the cardinal virtue to the Suhili, for without it, they would already be dead. To the Sogdans, who once dared to build a tower so tall that God smote them down and cursed them to never again work so boldly together, Temperance is the most important virtue. They must master themselves if they are ever to master their curse. And the Bandar, who have faced many years of drought, deprivation, and hardship, Generosity is the principal virtue, for one must always remember that the hard days may yet come again.

### QAMARISM

A full exploration of Qamarism would take many hundreds of pages and years of discussion and debate with learned teachers. But this section nevertheless attempts to summarize a few of its principles, as it is the force that unites the Cradle of the World and transforms it into the Caliphate. Qamarism is descended from the teachings of the Nameless Prophet. The collected sayings and writings of the Prophet are commonly collected into a single volume called simply the Word of the Prophet. Each follower of the Word is expected to do their best to learn and understand the Word themself, so there may be more copies of this book in the Caliphate than there are people. Qamarists believe that there is only one God, with many names and faces. God is a benevolent being who created the world for the benefit of humanity and the djinn. As God created both humans and djinn, the Word is intended for humans and djinn alike. There are many stories of imams (teachers) going forth to spread the word to the djinn. Imams who found them have not always returned. The Prophet taught that every person should be free to reach their best potential. Enslavement, binding by name magic, and every vice that destroys a person's potential are therefore forbidden. Each person must be free because each person is required in the great struggle against evil. Zeth, the Enemy, seeks to undo creation and must be opposed. Everyone stands alone before God. The individual alone is responsible for their decisions and their fate. After death, each person will be judged by two angels of God. If that person is righteous, they will proceed to Paradise. If they are wicked, they will be consigned to hellfire until their sins are burned away.

> **ONE GOD, OR MANY?**
>
> In the time of the Empire of Rempet, the people believed there were many gods, yet now Qamarists believe there is only one who has many aspects. The Prophet himself is silent on the matter, using the language of his time (of many gods) to teach to his people. While some people, and especially old stories, still refer to gods in the plural, the modern theological understanding among the Caliphate is that there is only one God.

### CURRENT CONCERNS

The current caliph, Harun al-Hakim, is not beloved by his subjects or his neighbors. He is not especially pious and is quite content to use his wealth and power to indulge his vices (mainly wine, sport, and gambling) while his advisors run his kingdom in his name. However, the caliph is wise enough to choose competent advisors and cunning enough to appear more foolish and lazy than he truly is. In truth, he is both ruthless and patient—a dangerous combination. The caliph's foremost concern is his fractious emirs. The rulers of the Caliphate's member states are each powerful in their own right, jealous of the caliph's wealth and power, and eager to increase their own standing. More than one would have already risen up in rebellion but for the caliph's simple and brutal strategy to control them: he has in his court one or more young people from every emir's family as hostages. Not quite prisoners, these "little birds" are kept in a gilded cage, given excellent educations and every luxury of al-Zawira's Inner City. Many neither notice nor begrudge their captivity and indeed look with favor upon the caliph, or even serve as advisors and bureaucrats in his court. And so, Harun al-Hakim ensures the begrudging loyalty of this generation of emirs and the eager friendship of the next. Even with his hostages, the caliph's position is not stable. He must rely on his religious authority to retain the loyalty of his populace, but as he is not an especially pious man himself, he must look for other opportunities to prove his worth as defender of the faith—certainly if he wishes to increase the Caliphate's, and therefore his own, wealth and power, he must find a religious excuse to do so. Fortunately for the caliph, such an excuse has presented itself in the form of the Unicorn Clan. Although the Unicorn are arguably responsible for the Caliphate's rise amid the ruins of Rempet, they are also perennial enemies. Twice before, the Unicorn and the Caliphate have gone to war, the Unicorn having ultimately been victorious in both instances. As a result, all the lands along the Sand Road east of al-Zawira are Unicorn territory—but they are also, largely, Qamarist. Since the caliph claims dominion over all the Qamarist people, that gives Harun all the justification he needs among the emirs to conquer the Unicorn lands throughout the Burning Sands. All he requires is some indication that the Unicorn are distracted…

### THE LANDS OF THE CALIPHATE

The Caliphate lies in the center of the world; it is the blooming garden in the heart of the desert, from which all of civilization was born. The people of this region refer to it as the Cradle of the World and believe that all of human history began here.

The Caliphate lies roughly between three seas: the Sea of Jewels in the south, the Conqueror's Sea to the west, and the terrible Cursed Sea to the north. As a result, the Cradle is the only overland route between dozens of faraway kingdoms, from Rokugan to the Mweneta Empire, and many more both large and small. No wonder the Unicorn Clan returned here so often during its long exile.

To the east of the Caliphate's lands lie the Burning Sands: endless dunes cooked hot by the merciless sun, spurs of red rocks cutting at the sky like the teeth of a gigantic dragon…and scarcely any water anywhere. It is said that in ancient times, many of the lands in this region, claimed by the Empire of Rempet, were fertile and blooming. But as the ancient empire grew in wickedness, the desert too began to grow, swallowing entire cities that now slumber beneath the sands. The Burning Sands are not, in general, claimed by any great empire—rather, they are endured by travelers moving from one side to the other. Within its expanse lie countless oases, some supporting small cities or entire civilizations. Ujik and other nomads ply these sands primarily on camels, always in search of shade and water. The Burning Sands form an effective bulwark against the Caliphate's potential rivals in the east, for marching an army across that trackless desert is a mighty feat, not often repeated.

> **THE CURSED SEA**
>
> Although the caliph is focused on new lands to conquer and wealth to add to his coffers, the Suhili stewards of Zymarkin have once again renewed their calls for aid from the rest of the Caliphate. The Cursed Sea boils, they claim, and monsters and dragons climb from its depths much more frequently than they did a generation before. There have even been sightings of warriors bearing the sigil of the cursed undead pharaoh, Ahtuthotep, the Betrayer of Horiz-Rem.

To the west of the Burning Sands lies the Cradle of the World proper, the land between two rivers, where every manner of fruit and vine blooms. The King's River forms its eastern border, abutting the Burning Sands. The river flows down from the Mountains of the Sun, twisting and turning until it empties through the Salt Flats into the Sea of Jewels. To the west, the Queen's River flows from both the Mountains of the Moon and the Mountains of the Sun; the two join into one mighty river somewhat southeast of the City of God. Flowing more or less in parallel to its sibling river to the east, the Queen's River ultimately also flows into the Sea of Jewels, fed the entire way from the highlands around the Mountains of the Moon. Between these rivers lie some of the most heavily irrigated and improved farmland in the world, the homeland of the Nehiri people, "the people of the rivers." Olives and figs; grapes, oranges, and pomegranates; and every kind of grain grow here in abundance—the shaded groves and gardens of the people are as beautiful and precious as any jewel.

Across the Mountains of the Sun from this fertile land lies the Conqueror's Coast, where the city-states of the Sogdans still rise. Cedar forests march from the mountain slopes to the forbidding cliffs overlooking the sea, and farms and villages cluster close to the massive walls of their parent cities. Although the Peace of the Prophet holds in these lands, and it has been many years since the Sogdans made war on one another, the memory of their ancient hostility still haunts the eaves of the forests, where bandits, djinn, and monsters dwell.

Each of the dozen city-states of the Sogdans has its own emir and its own character—even its own patron spirit, in ancient days. Nezer, the City of Stone, stands on the edge of a crystal lake in the mountains and is renowned for its quarries, architecture, and statues. Ninua, the City of Light, boasts the Caliphate's most impressive lighthouse in the form of a mighty ziggurat. Mashalukan, the City of the Serpent, nestles in a beautiful bay on the Conqueror's Coast. On the bluffs above the city, the ancient kings once chained criminals as a sacrifice to the terrible sea serpent that both protected and terrorized their city. Every Sogdan city-state has such stories and fame behind it. To the north lie the windswept plains that slope gently down to the still, black waters of the Cursed Sea. This is the home of the Suhili, the people of the plains, proud horse lords of the Caliphate. Despite the proximity of the Cursed Sea, the plains in this region are as fertile as any other and make excellent grazing lands for horses, cattle, goats, and sheep. Small streams that flow through the plains are shaded by enormous chenar trees, prized for their shade-giving crowns in the heat of the summer. Yet for all its charms, the area is feared and avoided by most Qamari—for it is where monsters dwell. The villages that do dot the area are crammed behind tall earthen walls, dwellings and workshops piled atop one another in the smallest footprint available as groves, fields, and farmlands surround them. The monsters that crawl from the sea are

> **THE TOWER OF HEAVEN AND THE CURSE OF DISHARMONY**
>
> In the most ancient past, the Sogdans were not divided into a dozen city-states as they are now. They worked together and built a mighty ziggurat, a tower so tall that its highest levels approached Heaven. The gods (or God) above were threatened by the tower. "See what mortal humans can do, united!" they cried. "They think to challenge the gods! We must not allow this!" And so in a single night of disaster, the gods smote the Tower of Heaven and broke the Sogdans into many warring city-states, so that they could never again threaten the gods. To this day, the Curse of Disharmony plagues the Sogdan people, and only the Word of the Prophet has the power to keep them united.

often swift, and even walls are no barrier to the drag-ons and other winged beasts, so Suhili go nowhere unarmed, and all their settlements are vigorously defended. Their mounted patrols ride swiftly to any word of a new monster, hunting and killing the beast as quickly as can be managed. Despite these precautions, everyone has at least one family member who has been slain by the servants of Zeth, and the hatred of the Drowned God burns brightly in the people here. To the west of Sogdan lands, a narrow isthmus is all that divides the Sea of Jewels and the Conqueror's Sea. Called the Djinni's Passage, it is a craggy, dry expanse that, it is said, once flooded at the approach of the greatest of all the sea dragons and serves as the grave of a mighty marid. South of the Djinni's Passage, along the western coast of the Sea of Jewels, lie the lands of the Bandar. Rocky hills rise swiftly from the coast, with many fertile valleys lying between them. Above these lie the Bandar Highlands, where sudden drops reveal tiny lakes like mirrors or surprising rivers flowing beneath arches of red stone. The grandest of these rivers, the Serpent River, flows north and joins the mighty Beryt on its course toward the Conqueror's Sea. The coastal Bandar farm their valleys, fish extensively, and sail far and wide in search of treasure and trade. From the great city of Mozedu, blessed with the finest harbor in the Caliphate, Bandar vessels seek out the Mweneta Empire, Ivory Kingdoms, Pavarre, Mantis Clan, and dozens of other trade partners all around the world. The inland Bandar of the highlands raise goats, hunt and fish their rivers, and mine precious stones and gems from their hills to trade with their coastal and riverine cousins, as well as with the people of the Beryt.

### AL-ZAWIRA, THE CITY OF BOOKS AND THE SEAT OF THE CALIPH

Al-Zawira is not the most holy city in the Caliphate, nor necessarily the largest, nor even the richest. And yet it has always been counted foremost among the great cities of the Qamari and has been the home of the caliph more often than not, as indeed it is today. It commands a fine location in a crook of the King's River, and the trade across the Unicorn's Sand Road terminates in the city, so it is certainly wealthy enough. It is beautiful, with its famous Round City and Pilgrim's Court…yet it is the Court of Wisdom that is its true heart.

Al-Zawira has long been the center of scholarship, mathematics, and natural philosophy in the Caliphate, and these virtues have been prized by many caliphs across the centuries. The famous Round City is celebrated not for its impressive height (although its walls are grand) but for the perfection of its circles, one within the other. Many of the grandest palaces, gardens, and markets lie behind its walls, including the famous Market of Books and the Inner City, which is the true heart of the caliph's power. The Court of Wisdom, on the other hand, lies at a modest remove from the Round City—perhaps due to the more dangerous experiments undertaken by its residents in years past. Here in this many-columned palace, the caliph sponsors over a dozen brilliant scholars in their research. Perhaps even now they are perfecting some new technology that will allow the Caliphate to triumph over its enemies.

### ZYMARKIN, THE CITY OF THE BRONZE GATE

The only city of the Suhili people, Zymarkin is the great bastion against the forces of Zeth. The fortress city is surrounded by grand walls of black stone, with great bronze gates wrought with scenes of struggle and triumph, and some say magic spells. The grand gates are a replacement for previous doors of wood, which legend says burned when the city was sacked by the Enemy during the spread of the Word. Only the arrival of the Prophet and his armies saved the people of Zymarkin from a fate worse than death, and so the Suhili joined the Caliphate.

Today, Zymarkin rings with the sound of hammers on anvils and roars with the heat of forges. It is here that some of the Caliphate's finest weapons, the famous "watered steel," are made from ingots wrapped in clay carried from the nearby Cursed Sea. The Brass Bull of Zymarkin stands guard at the city's northern gate, where generations of warriors have kissed its nose for luck, leaving it brightly polished while the rest of the great statue slowly tarnishes with age.

#### Saadiyah bin Abdul Rahim al-Mozedu

Perhaps the most valued, and valuable, of the caliph's "little birds" is Saadiyah bin Abdul Rahim al-Mozedu. The daughter of the most powerful of the Bandar emirs, Saadiyah has been a hostage in the caliph's court since she was a girl. Now that she is an adult, Saadiyah has matured into an intensely intelligent and surprisingly pious woman who has become one of the caliph's most valued advisors.

Even more so than her fellow hostages, Saadiyah has taken every advantage of the indulgences the caliph has allowed them. She had access to the finest tutors in the Caliphate and learned everything she could from them, and as a consequence, she is now one of the most promising scientific minds in al-Zawira. She longs to be permitted to enter the Court of Wisdom, where she could finally achieve her potential in mathematics and the sciences. But even more, she longs to escape her captivity and allow her father to finally act freely to oppose the caliph.

She is not inclined to trust foreigners and heathens, and her hatred of the caliph does not extend to betraying the Caliphate—but she is also keen to find allies. PCs who can convince her that they have the best interests of the Qamari people at heart could find Saadiyah a valuable ally. Those who oppose the Caliphate may find her a surprisingly inconvenient enemy.
